The paragraph discusses the importance of Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search in various fields and its growing significance with large language models, particularly for retrieval-augmented generation pipelines. It mentions that any vector database system implements some form of ANN search to enable efficient vector similarity queries. The paragraph also highlights the use of graph-based approaches like Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs and their empirical performance, as well as theoretical guarantees provided by hash-based methods. It contrasts these with quantization-based methods that trade off speed for approximation error. Additionally, it mentions deterministic methods based on discrepancy theory but notes they are impractical in high dimensions. The paragraph concludes by introducing a new class of hierarchical graphs grounded in \ud835\udf00-net theory and compares them to existing ANN graphs like kNN, NSW, NSG, RNG, etc., while also referencing the practicality issues with HNSW.
[citation_split] paragraph_id=Introduction::p1 sample=4 attempt=2/5
You have provided a paragraph discussing the importance of Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search in various fields and its applications, particularly in large language models (LLMs). The text also mentions that ANN is crucial for retrieval-augmented generation pipelines. It highlights that while hash-based approaches offer theoretical guarantees on retrieval quality, they often struggle with practical implementations. Quantization methods are mentioned as a trade-off between speed and accuracy due to approximation errors but provide significant speedups. The paragraph also references the use of graph-based ANN algorithms like kNN, NSW (Navigable Small World), NSG, RNG, etc., which have gained attention for their empirical performance and scalability. It introduces a new class of hierarchical graphs based on \ud835\udc5f-net theory from computational geometry to improve ANN search efficiency.
The paragraph also discusses the Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graph, which is widely used in practice but lacks formal query time guarantees and has a linear worst-case complexity with respect to dataset size in adversarial settings. In contrast, Cover Trees are mentioned as providing logarithmic query time guarantees using \ud835\udf00-nets from computational geometry theory.

The paragraph also introduces the introduction of HENN (Hierarchical Explicit Nearest Neighbor) as a new index in Faiss library, comparing it to other baselines and validating its theoretical claims empirically. The text concludes by mentioning that while HNSW is easy to implement and widely used but lacks formal guarantees on query time, deterministic methods based on discrepancy theory are impractical for high-dimensional data.

